sycl: overlap the K/V staging with compute in the DPAS decode flash-attention kernel

HomeBrain patch on ggml-sycl. Applies on top of 0024.
Upstream tracking: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/issues/32757

The DPAS decode kernel for q8_0 K/V at head size 128 (Inkling's
full-attention layers) staged each 256-key tile into SLM by dword loads,
then waited at a barrier, for K before Q.K and again for V before P.V. The
loads never overlapped compute: with the phases switched off one at a time,
the 65,536-key decode case split into about 700 us of loads and 700 us of
everything else. For one row tile (up to 16 query rows) the kernel now
issues each tile's loads into registers one phase early (V during Q.K, the
next K during the softmax and P.V) and stores them to SLM after the barrier
that frees the tile; the L1 prefetches are dropped. P.V takes whole V
blocks: four sub-groups share a block and split its key groups, so each
block is read from SLM once instead of four times, and they add their O once
at the end. The kernel holds a tile in flight in the 256-register file,
which fits two work-groups per Xe2 core, and the key split targets two.
test-backend-ops on the Arc Pro B60, Inkling's decode attention (8 KV heads,
GQA 3, indexed bias, sinks): 28.4 -> 25.4 us at 1,024 keys, 99.3 -> 66.8 us
at 4,096, 353 -> 214 us at 16,384 and 1,236 -> 832 us at 65,536. The
llama.cpp branch adds a test-backend-ops sweep of q8_0 head-size-128 decode
at 1 to 20,001 keys, 1, 2 and 5 queries, GQA 1, 3, 8 and 16, with and
without mask and sinks.

